@doc smysl/0.1 {
  id: v/f2
  intent: research-trace
  lang: en
  granularity: { profile: fine }
  roots: [f/dose-response]
}

@definition d/effect-size { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "methods-handbook#effect-size" } }
~ Standardised mean difference between treatment and control arms.

@evidence e/cohort-a { status: measured, source: { kind: file, ref: "cohort-a/2026-04", captured: 2026-05-02 } }
~ Cohort A, 1 240 subjects: effect size 0.41, confidence interval 0.29 to 0.53.

The pre-registered primary endpoint, analysed by the plan filed before
enrolment opened and with no interim look at the data.

@evidence e/cohort-b { status: measured, source: { kind: file, ref: "cohort-b/2026-04", captured: 2026-05-11 } }
~ Cohort B, 980 subjects: effect size 0.38, confidence interval 0.24 to 0.52.

An independent site running the same protocol, recruited from a different
registry and analysed by a team blind to cohort A's result.

@evidence e/dose-record { status: measured, source: { kind: file, ref: "pharmacy.dispense", captured: 2026-05-11 } }
~ Dispensing logs recording administered dose per subject per week.

@claim c/replicates { status: derived, grounds: [e/cohort-a, e/cohort-b], deps: [d/effect-size] }
~ The effect replicates across two independently recruited cohorts.

Two sites, two registries, overlapping confidence intervals and a combined
estimate that moves very little from either arm taken alone.

@claim c/dose-response { status: derived, grounds: [e/dose-record, e/cohort-a] }
~ Response rises monotonically with administered dose.

@claim c/mechanism { status: inferred, grounds: [c/replicates, c/dose-response] }
~ A dose-dependent pharmacological mechanism is the most economical account.

Replication rules out a site artefact and the dose gradient rules out a pure
placebo response, which together leave the pharmacological reading.

@hypothesis h/adherence { status: speculative }
~ Differential adherence could produce the same dose gradient.

Subjects who tolerate the drug take more of it and are also plausibly the
subjects likelier to improve, which would bend the curve without any
pharmacological effect at all.

@evidence e/adherence-flat { status: measured, source: { kind: file, ref: "cohort-a/adherence", captured: 2026-05-02 } }
~ Adherence was 94 per cent or better in every dose band.

@claim c/adherence-ruled-out { status: derived, grounds: [e/adherence-flat] }
~ Adherence does not vary enough across bands to explain the gradient.

@finding f/dose-response { status: inferred, grounds: [c/mechanism, c/adherence-ruled-out] }
~ The evidence supports a genuine dose-dependent effect of moderate size.

@rel e/cohort-b --backs--> c/replicates
@rel c/adherence-ruled-out --rebuts--> h/adherence { weight: 0.8 }
@rel h/adherence --contrasts--> c/mechanism
@rel d/effect-size --warrant--> c/replicates
@rel c/dose-response --causes--> c/mechanism
